.TH SZ 1 2.6.1996 lrzsz-0.12b
.SH NAME
sx, sb, sz \- XMODEM, YMODEM, ZMODEM file send
.SH SYNOPSIS
sz
.RB [\- +8abdefkLlNnopqTtuvyY ]
.I file ...
.br
sb
.RB [\- adfkqtuv ]
.I file ...
.br
sx
.RB [\- akqtuv ]
.I file
.br
sz
.RB [\- oqtv ]
.B "-c COMMAND"
.br
sz
.RB [\- oqtv ]
.B "-i COMMAND"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Sz
uses the ZMODEM, YMODEM or XMODEM error correcting protocol to send
one or more files over a dial-in serial port to a variety of programs running under
multiple operating systems.

.B Sz
sends one or more files with ZMODEM protocol.

ZMODEM
greatly simplifies file transfers compared to XMODEM.
In addition to a friendly user interface, ZMODEM
provides Personal Computer and other users
an efficient, accurate, and robust file transfer method.

ZMODEM provides complete
.B "END-TO-END"
data integrity between application programs.
ZMODEM's 32 bit CRC catches errors
that sneak into even the most advanced networks.

Advanced file management features include
AutoDownload (Automatic file Download initiated without user intervention),
Display of individual and total file lengths and transmission time estimates,
Crash Recovery,
selective file transfers,
and preservation of
exact file date and length.

Output from another program may be piped to
.B sz
for transmission by denoting standard input with "-":
.ce
ls -l | sz -
The program output is transmitted with the filename sPID.sz
where PID is the process ID of the
.B sz
program.
If the environment variable
.B ONAME
is set, that is used instead.
In this case, the Unix command:
.ce
ls -l | ONAME=con sz -ay -
will send a "file" to the PC-DOS console display.
The
.B -y
option instructs the receiver to open the file for writing unconditionally.
The
.B -a
option
causes the receiver to convert Unix newlines to PC-DOS carriage returns
and linefeeds.


.B Sb
batch sends one or more files with YMODEM or ZMODEM protocol.
The initial ZMODEM initialization is not sent.
When requested by the receiver (!!!),
.B sb
supports
.B YMODEM-g
with "cbreak" tty mode, XON/XOFF flow control,
and interrupt character set to CAN (^X). This 
increases throughput over error free channels
(direct connection, X.PC, etc.)
by not acknowledging each transmitted sector.

The receiver included in this package does not request YMODEM-g.

On
.SM Unix
systems, additional information about the file is transmitted.
If the receiving program uses this information,
the transmitted file length controls the exact number of bytes written to
the output dataset,
and the modify time and file mode
are set accordingly.


.B Sx
sends a single
.I file
with
.B XMODEM
or
.B XMODEM-1k
protocol
(sometimes incorrectly called "ymodem").
The user must supply the file name to both sending and receiving programs.

If
.B sz
is invoked with $SHELL set and if that variable contains the
string
.I "rsh"
,
.I "rbash"
or
.I "rksh"
(restricted shell),
.B sz
operates in restricted mode.
Restricted mode restricts pathnames to the current directory and/or 
subdirectories thereof.


The fourth form sends a single COMMAND to a ZMODEM receiver for execution.
.B Sz
exits with the COMMAND return value.
If COMMAND includes spaces or characters special to the shell,
it must be quoted.


The fifth form sends a single COMMAND to a ZMODEM receiver for execution.
.B Sz
exits as soon as the receiver has correctly received the command,
before it is executed.
This form (and thus the
.BR \-c / \-i
options) is deprecated and will be removed in a future release: it
asks the receiving program to execute COMMAND, which only old ZMODEM
implementations still do.


If
.B sz
is invoked with stdout and stderr to different datasets,
Verbose is set to 2, causing frame by frame progress reports
to stderr.
This may be disabled with the
.B q
option.
.PP
The meanings of the available options are:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP
.B "-+, --append"
Instruct the receiver to append transmitted data to an existing file
(ZMODEM only).
.TP 
.B "-2, --twostop"
use two stop bits (if possible). Do not use this unless you know
what you are doing.
.TP
.B "-8, --try-8k"
Try to go up to 8KB blocksize. This is incompatible with standard zmodem,
but a common extension in the bbs world. (ZMODEM only).
.TP
.B "-9"
Short form of --start-8k.
.TP
.B "--start-8k"
Start with 8KB blocksize. Like --try-8k.
.TP
.B "-4, --try-4k"
Try to go up to 4KB blocksize. (ZMODEM only).
.TP
.B "-5"
Short form of --start-4k.
.TP
.B "--start-4k"
Start with 4KB blocksize. Like --try-4k.
.TP
.B "-a, --ascii"
Convert NL characters in the transmitted file to CR/LF.
This is done by the sender for XMODEM and YMODEM, by the receiver
for ZMODEM.

Deprecated: for ZMODEM this requests the historic ZCNL conversion,
which current ZMODEM documentation marks as deprecated. It is kept
for compatibility with old peers; new setups should use binary
transfer.
.TP
.B "-b, --binary"
(ZMODEM) Binary override: transfer file without any translation.
.TP
.B "-B NUMBER, --bufsize NUMBER"
Use a readbuffer of 
.B NUMBER
bytes. Default ist 16384, which should be enough
for most situations. If you have a slow machine or a bad disk interface
or suffer from other hardware problems you might want to increase
the buffersize.
.B -1
or
.B auto
use a buffer large enough to buffer the whole file. Be careful with this
option - things normally get worse, not better, if the machine starts
to swap.

 .TP
.B "-c COMMAND, --command COMMAND"
Send COMMAND to the receiver for execution, return with COMMAND\'s exit status.
Deprecated: this asks the
.B receiving
program to execute COMMAND. It is kept for compatibility with old
ZMODEM implementations (OMEN zmodem and older
.BR lrz )
that still execute received commands, and will be removed in a future
release. New setups should not use it; modern receivers
(including lrz) never execute commands and answer with a failure status.
.TP
.B "-C N, --command-tries N"
Retry to send command N times (default: 11). Only meaningful together
with
.BR \-c / \-i ,
which are deprecated.
.TP
.B "--delay-startup N"
Wait
.B N
seconds before doing anything.
.TP
.B "--no-unixmode"
Send a mode field of 0 in the ZMODEM file header instead of the
file's Unix permission bits.
By default,
.B lsz
transmits the full
.I st_mode
as returned by
.BR fstat (2),
including permission bits (rwx) and setuid, setgid, and sticky bits.
The recipient may, and likely will, modify the mode before applying it
\(em for example,
.I lrz
masks the received mode with
.B 0777
in normal operation (stripping setuid, setgid, and sticky) and with
.B 0666
under restricted shell (also stripping execute bits).
Use
.B --no-unixmode
to leave permission selection entirely to the receiver's defaults 
(or to keep that information for yourself).
.TP 
.B "-e, --escape"
Escape all control characters;
normally XON, XOFF, DLE, CR-@-CR, and Ctrl-X are escaped.
.TP
.B "-E, --rename"
Force the sender to rename the new file if a file with the same
name already exists.
.TP
.B "-f, --full-path"
Send Full pathname.
Normally directory prefixes are stripped from the transmitted
filename.
Deprecated: the current ZMODEM documentation states that a sender
MUST NOT transmit absolute pathnames or
.B ..
components. This option is kept for compatibility with the historical
behavior (OMEN
.B sz
and derivatives, and for mirroring directory trees with relative
paths); a future release will continue to allow relative paths but
will reject absolute paths and
.B ..
components with a fatal error. New setups should use plain relative
filenames.
.TP
.B "-h, --help"
give help.
.TP
.B "-H, --crc-check"
(ZMODEM) Accept and transfer files if the file lengths and/or the CRCs
differ.
.TP
.B "-i COMMAND, --immediate-command COMMAND"
Send COMMAND to the receiver for execution, return immediately
upon the receiving program's successful reception of the command.
Deprecated: for the same reasons as
.BR \-c ;
kept for compatibility with old ZMODEM implementations that still
execute received commands, will be removed in a future release.
.TP
.B "-J FILE, --journal FILE"
Append a transfer-statistics line to
.B FILE
for each file sent.
The line consists of key=value pairs including timestamp, direction
(dir=tx), filename, status, byte counts, duration, and throughput.
The file is created if it does not exist and appended to on each
invocation.
.TP
.B "-k, --1k"
(XMODEM/YMODEM) Send files using 1024 byte blocks
rather than the default 128 byte blocks.
1024 byte packets speed file transfers at high bit rates.
(ZMODEM streams the data for the best possible throughput.)
.TP
.B "-L N, --packetlen N"
Use ZMODEM sub-packets of length N.
A larger N (32 <= N <= 1024) gives slightly higher throughput,
a smaller N speeds error recovery.
The default is 128 below 300 baud, 256 above 300 baud, or 1024 above 2400 baud.
.TP 
.B "-m N, --min-bps N"
Stop transmission if BPS-Rate (Bytes Per Second) falls below N for a
certain time (see --min-bps-time option).
The check is per file: the timer resets at the start of each file.
.TP
.B "-M N, --min-bps-time"
Used together with --min-bps. Default is 120 (seconds).
The timeout is per file: a slow transfer of one file does not count
toward the next file's timeout.
.TP
.B "-l N, --framelen N"
Wait for the receiver to acknowledge correct data every
.B N
(32 <= N <= 1024)
characters.
This may be used to avoid network overrun when XOFF flow control is lacking.
.TP
.B "--sync-transfer"
(ZMODEM) Integrity mode: wait for the receiver's acknowledgement after
every data sub-packet (a ZCRCW round trip), instead of streaming.
On a packetized transport an entire sub-packet together with its
terminator and CRC can be silently swallowed, which plain streaming
cannot detect (the CRC is position independent and the frame
position is only checked at the start of a ZDATA frame).
The cost is one round trip per sub-packet, causing a substantial
throughput loss on slow or high-latency lines.
This option is only needed on transports that can silently drop a
contiguous chunk of the byte stream, for example a packet radio or
other datagram link that loses packets without retransmitting them,
or a line that overruns its buffer when flow control fails. It is pointless on
transports that hide packet loss by retransmitting internally, so that
no loss reaches the byte stream (TCP, ISDN, X.25), and wasteful on
transports that only corrupt individual characters (noisy modem or
serial lines): there the CRC catches the damage and the normal resync
heals it, streaming is safe and faster.
.TP
.B "-n, --newer"
(ZMODEM) Send each file if
destination file does not exist.
Overwrite destination file if
source file is newer than the destination file.
.TP
.B "-N, --newer-or-longer"
(ZMODEM) Send each file if
destination file does not exist.
Overwrite destination file if
source file is newer or longer than the destination file.
.TP
.B "-o, --16-bit-crc"
(ZMODEM) Disable automatic selection of 32 bit CRC.
.TP
.B "-O, --disable-timeouts"
Disable read timeout handling. This makes lsz hang if the other side
doesn't send anything, but increases performance (not much) and
decreases system load (reduces number of system calls by about 50
percent).

Use this option with care.
.TP
.B "-p, --protect"
(ZMODEM) Protect existing destination files by skipping transfer if the
destination file exists.
.TP
.B "-q, --quiet"
Quiet suppresses verbosity.
.TP
.B "-R, --restricted"
Restricted mode: restricts pathnames to the current directory
and/or subdirectories thereof. See
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.B "-r, --resume"
(ZMODEM) Resume interrupted file transfer.
If the source file is longer than the destination file,
the transfer commences at the offset in the source file that equals
the length of the destination file.
.TP
.B "-s HH:MM, --stop-at HH:MM"
Stop transmission at
.B HH
hours,
.B MM
minutes. Another variant, using
.B +N
instead of
.B HH:MM,
stops transmission in
.B N
seconds. The deadline is tracked with a one-second granularity because
of the way the check is implemented (the
.B N
argument is parsed before any data flows and compared against integer
seconds afterwards); the transfer stops during the second after the
deadline is reached, so a transfer stops within about
.B N+1
to
.B N+2
seconds.
.TP
.B "--syslog[=off]"
turn syslogging on or off. Syslog is on by default.
.TP
.B "-t TIM, --timeout TIM"
Change timeout to
.I TIM
tenths of seconds.
.TP
.B "-T, --turbo"
Do not escape certain characters (^P, ^P|0x80, telenet escape sequence
[CR + @]). This improves performance by about 1 percent and shouldn't
hurt in the normal case (but be careful - ^P might be useful if connected
through a terminal server).

.TP
.B "-u, --unlink"
Unlink the file after successful transmission.
.TP
.B "-U, --unrestrict"
Removed. See
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.B "-w N, --windowsize N"
Limit the transmit window size to N bytes (ZMODEM).
.TP
.B "-v, --verbose"
Verbose output to stderr. With one
.BR v ,
a summary (file names, transfer result, error events) is printed. With two
.BR v s,
progress reports are also shown. More
.BR v s
are accepted but have no effect. Since version 0.13 detailed protocol 
debugging is available with
.B \-\-debug
instead.
.TP
.B "\-\-debug=MODULES"
Enable debug output for the comma separated list of MODULES on stderr.
Known modules: "protocol" (ZMODEM frame state machine), "readline" (line
reader: select/read calls, timeouts, hex dump), "transfer" (file transfer
state machine), "windowhandling" (adaptive block length and window
logic, ZMODEM only), "tty" (terminal mode changes). The special name
"all" enables all.
.TP
.B "-X, --xmodem"
use XMODEM protocol.
.TP
.B "-y, --overwrite"
Instruct a ZMODEM receiving program to overwrite any existing file
with the same name.
.TP
.B "-Y, --overwrite-or-skip"
Instruct a ZMODEM receiving program to overwrite any existing file
with the same name,
and to skip any source files that do have a file with the same
pathname on the destination system.
.TP
.B "-g, --ymodem"
use ZMODEM protocol.
.TP
.B "-Z, --zmodem"
use ZMODEM protocol.
.PD
.SH SECURITY
Restricted mode restricts pathnames to the current directory
and/or subdirectories thereof: absolute pathnames and references to
a parent directory are not sent, and lsz refuses to send remote
commands (the \-c/\-i options).

Restricted mode is entered if the
.B R
option is given or if lsz detects that it runs under a restricted
shell or if the environment variable
.B ZMODEM_RESTRICTED
is set.
Restricted mode cannot be turned off; the
.B U
option has been removed (see
.BR "HISTORICAL NOTES" ).

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ZNULLS
may be used to specify the number of nulls to send before a ZDATA frame. This is a 1980s modem workaround and of questionable benefit today. It is kept for compatibility.
.TP
.B SHELL
lsz recognizes a restricted shell if this variable includes
.I "rsh"
or
.I "rksh"
.TP
.B ZMODEM_RESTRICTED
lsz enters restricted mode if the variable is set.
.SH EXAMPLES
.ne 7
.B "ZMODEM File Transfer"
.br
.B "% sz \-a *.c"
.br
This single command transfers all .c files in the current directory
with conversion
.RB ( \-a )
to end of line conventions appropriate to the receiving environment.

.br
.B "% sz \-Yan *.c *.h"
.br
Send only the .c and .h files that exist on both systems,
and are newer on the sending system than the
corresponding version on the receiving system, converting Unix to
DOS text format if needed.

.B "XMODEM File Transfer"
.br
%
.B "sx \-a foo.c"
.br
.B "ESC"
.br
.B "rx foo.c"
.br
The above three commands transfer a single file
from the local to the remote machine while
.I sz
translating Unix newlines to DOS CR/LF.
This combination is much slower and far less reliable than ZMODEM.
.SH ERROR MESSAGES
"Caught signal 99"
indicates the program was not properly compiled,
refer to "bibi(99)" in rbsb.c for details.
.SH SEE ALSO
lrz,
ZMODEM.DOC,
YMODEM.DOC,

Compile time options required for various operating systems are described in
the source file.

.SH BUGS
On at least one BSD system, sz would hang or exit when it got within
a few kilobytes of the end of file.
Using the "-w 8192" flag fixed the problem.
The real cause is unknown, perhaps a bug in the kernel TTY output routines.

Programs that do not properly implement the specified file transfer protocol
may cause
.I sz
to "hang" the port for a minute or two.

Many programs claiming to support YMODEM only support XMODEM with 1k blocks,
and they often don't get that quite right.

XMODEM transfers add up to 127 garbage bytes per file.
XMODEM-1k and YMODEM-1k transfers use 128 byte blocks
to avoid extra padding.

YMODEM programs use the file length transmitted at the beginning of the
transfer to prune the file to the correct length; this may cause problems with
source files that grow during the course of the transfer.
This problem does not pertain to ZMODEM transfers, which preserve the exact
file length unconditionally.

Most ZMODEM options are merely passed to the receiving program;
some do not implement all these options.

Circular buffering and a ZMODEM sliding window should be used
when input is from pipes instead of acknowledging frames each 1024 bytes.
If no files can be opened,
.B sz
sends a ZMODEM command to echo a suitable complaint;
perhaps it should check for the presence of at least one accessible file before
getting hot and bothered.

.SH WORKAROUNDS
A few high speed modems have (had?) a firmware bug that drops characters when the
direction of high speed transmission is reversed.
The environment variable ZNULLS may be used to specify the number of nulls to
send before a ZDATA frame.
Values of 101 for a 4.77 MHz PC and 124 for an AT were typical. 
.SH CAVEATS
The ZMODEM protocol uses 32-bit fields for file positions, imposing a
hard limit of 4 GiB on transfer size.
Furthermore, many ZMODEM implementations derived from the original
Omen Technology codebase (including
.IR crzsz ,
.IR rzsz ,
.IR lrzsz older than version 0.13,
and other 1990s variants) and also at least some independent
implementation use signed
.I long
internally, which on 32-bit platforms limits correct operation to
files below 2 GiB.
.B lsz
itself handles positions up to 4 GiB correctly, but interoperability
with older or 32-bit receivers may fail for files larger than 2 GiB.
.B lsz
prints a warning when sending files larger than 2 GiB; use
.B -q
to suppress it.
.SH "HISTORICAL NOTES"
When a receiver ends the session with a ZABORT frame (or ZFERR, which
the ZMODEM documentation defines as equivalent),
.B lsz
replies with the ZFIN sequence the documentation requires and then ends
the session cleanly. Older lsz versions, as well as OMEN-derived senders 
answered with the cancel sequence instead. The outcome (transfer aborted, 
exit status 0x80) is the same, so this only matters to peers that inspect 
the reply.
.TP
The following options and features have been removed and are 
no longer functional. Using the options prints an error message and exits.
.TP
.B "-S, --timesync"
Time synchronization.
Removed because no other still-available implementation supported it,
and setting the local time would have required root privileges.
.TP
.B "-U, --unrestrict"
Turned off restricted mode (impossible under a restricted shell).
Removed because security-relevant options cannot be turned off:
whoever can pass \-U can equally well drop it from the command line,
so the option provided no protection — only a trap. See
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.B PUBDIR
The
.B PUBDIR
environment variable and the associated public directory feature
(restricting file transfers to
.IR PUBDIR ,
usually
.IR /var/spool/uucppublic )
have been removed.
.TP
.B "-d, --dot-to-slash"
Converted dots to slashes in pathnames and truncated filenames to
8.3 format for MS-DOS / CP-M compatibility.
Removed because these systems are obsolete.
Use
.B -f / --full-path
to send full pathnames.
